.TH G-UNITY7-CLEANUP 8 "November 2026" "g-unity7-desktop" "G Unity7"
.SH NAME
g-unity7-cleanup \- remove the Ubuntu Unity packages that G Unity7 replaces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B g-unity7-cleanup
.RB [ \-n | \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-y | \-\-yes ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B g-unity7-cleanup
removes duplicate Ubuntu Unity applications and other installed alternatives
of the
.B g-unity7-desktop
applications, like Gentoo's
.BR "emerge \-\-depclean" .
.PP
The Ubuntu Unity installer installs the
.B ubuntu-unity-desktop
task, and apt marks its packages as manually installed, so
.B apt autoremove
never removes them. apt also keeps every installed alternative of a relation
such as
.BR "vlc | showtime" .
.PP
For each
.B g-unity7-desktop
relation that has several installed alternatives, such as
.BR "atril | papers | evince" ,
the script asks which package to keep. It also asks when a different
alternative is installed instead of the first one, because apt does not
install
.B eog
from
.B "eog | eom | loupe"
when
.B eom
is installed. You can keep the first alternative (recommended) or another one,
which is installed if needed. When several alternatives are installed, you can
also leave them all unchanged. A kept first alternative is marked as
automatically installed, and any other kept alternative stays manually
installed. The other installed alternatives are removed, including those you
installed yourself, unless other packages need them.
.PP
The script then marks
.BR ubuntu-unity-desktop ,
the packages it depends on or recommends that
.B g-unity7-desktop
does not use, and the task packages that these packages and the removed
alternatives depend on (such as
.B file-roller
for
.BR nemo-fileroller )
as automatically installed, and removes them with autoremove. Packages kept by
a choice are not marked. Only packages that autoremove actually removes are
marked. Packages that are still needed, for example by
.B ubuntu-desktop
on Ubuntu, which shares most of the task, are left as they are.
Packages that
.B ubuntu-unity-desktop
depends on or recommends and that apt keeps only as an installed alternative
of another relation, such as
.B lightdm-gtk-greeter
for
.BR lightdm-greeter ,
are removed directly if no other package is removed with them.
.PP
The Ubuntu Firefox and Thunderbird packages install snaps. The G Unity⁷ PPA
provides them as regular packages with Unity's global menu, built from the
Debian sources. Their version is lower, so
.B g-unity7-apt-pin
keeps the Ubuntu packages until the script installs the G Unity⁷ packages. If
a snap is installed, the script asks whether to install the G Unity⁷ package
instead, together with its language packages for the languages in use
.RB ( LANG
and
.B LANGUAGE
in
.IR /etc/default/locale ,
and the language that each user chose).
It then copies the snap profile
.RI ( ~/snap/firefox/common/.mozilla/firefox ,
.IR ~/snap/thunderbird/common/.thunderbird )
for every user who does not have a profile yet
.RI ( ~/.mozilla/firefox ,
.IR ~/.thunderbird ),
makes the snap's default profile the default profile of the new package, and
removes the snap. snapd saves a snapshot of the snap data. A running snap is
not removed. Close the application and run the script again. Run it again
also if removing the snap fails. A G Unity⁷ package older than the snap is
not offered, because Firefox and Thunderbird do not open a profile that a newer
version used. Run the script again after the package is updated.
.PP
The script shows all changes and asks for confirmation. To keep a package,
mark it as manually installed first:
.BI "sudo apt-mark manual " package
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show the changes without making them. The script still asks about the
alternatives, so that the list shows the result of your answers. Root is not
needed.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask. Keep or install the recommended alternative of each relation and
make the changes without confirmation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apt (8),
.BR apt-mark (8)
